<http://data.silknow.org/image/cdf713e5-fbd8-3d87-8153-d8622a4c6791>
        a                  ecrm:E38_Image ;
        dc:identifier      "2012FH8715" ;
        schema:contentUrl  <https://silknow.org/silknow/media/vam/O80583_1.jpg> , <http://media.vam.ac.uk/media/thira/collection_images/2012FH/2012FH8715.jpg> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/4a158d32-0d29-30ae-9b83-6d2a91503e48>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Tapestry fragment woven in wool and silk, Sheldon Tapestry Workshops, possibly made in Warwickshire, ca. 1588"@en , "Tapestry fragment woven in wool and silk. 16-17 warp threads per in (6-7 per cm). Part of the lower right hand edge of the tapestry map of Oxford and Berkshire, and showing the coat of arms of Ralph Sheldon Senior (1537-1613), and the family motto 'OPTIMUM PATI'. In the border is a wild boar and the tip of Hercules' club which is visible in the right border."@en , "Maps used as designs for tapestry wall hangings are uncommon. Only three other 16th century examples are known; a map of Paris woven around 1540 and one of Leiden in 1584. A set of twelve showing the progress of the Armada up the English Channel in 1588 was commissioned by Charles Howard, Lord Admiral of England at the time, from workshops in Delft in 1596.  Only that of Leiden survives, in the Lakenhal in Leiden.\nRalph Sheldon’s four tapestry maps of English counties were probably ordered for the new house he completed at Weston near Long Compton, Warwickshire around 1590. Focussed on the counties of Oxfordshire with Berkshire, Warwickshire, Worcestershire and Gloucestershire, the set’s total length of 21 metres depicted a view across England from London to the Bristol Channel. This splendid panorama, 4 metres high, reflected not only Ralph’s known interest in maps but also the excitement created by the first maps of English counties published by Christopher Saxton as an Atlas in 1579.\nTo bring those maps alive the tapestry designer replaced symbols with pictures and added or emphasized details. The gates and palings of parkland stand out; clusters of houses represented villages; windmills, beacons and bridges close to Sheldon properties were added. Towns and some of the large houses, including Sheldon’s own and those of his relatives, were depicted as they really looked. Hills and rivers portrayed the landscape across which wandered roads, which Saxton  had not included. Only trees were drawn out of scale, often shown taller than the church towers and spires.\nThe original design, the same for every tapestry, is made clear by the only intact survival, hanging in the Warwickshire Museum, Warwick.  In the lower right-hand corner were the Sheldon family arms; above was text based on William Camden’s best-selling description of Britain, the Britannia.  The corners of the left-hand side showed the royal arms and a scale and dividers. A variety of designs borrowed from printed sources made up the borders, including flowers, canopies, columns and the figures of Hercules, Temperance and Mercury. A map of Africa was inserted into the lower border.  Doggerel verses described the county and the orientation appeared in each border.\nOwnership and Use\nThe four tapestries, which probably took several years to produce, created a unique decorative scheme, amongst the finest of the time. They may have been damaged when parliamentary forces attacked the royalist Sheldons’ house in the Civil War (1642-48).  Much later, these pieces were deliberately cut from the lower right-hand corner to decorate a fire screen."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/0051d9f9-5a78-3c69-9f0f-d09110aa8e56> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/40b6e4fb-91fb-3431-a8cb-3cda33ca33da> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/cdf713e5-fbd8-3d87-8153-d8622a4c6791> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Maps used as designs for tapestry wall hangings are uncommon. Only three other 16th century examples are known; a map of Paris woven around 1540 and one of Leiden in 1584. A set of twelve showing the progress of the Armada up the English Channel in 1588 was commissioned by Charles Howard, Lord Admiral of England at the time, from workshops in Delft in 1596.  Only that of Leiden survives, in the Lakenhal in Leiden.\nRalph Sheldon’s four tapestry maps of English counties were probably ordered for the new house he completed at Weston near Long Compton, Warwickshire around 1590. Focussed on the counties of Oxfordshire with Berkshire, Warwickshire, Worcestershire and Gloucestershire, the set’s total length of 21 metres depicted a view across England from London to the Bristol Channel. This splendid panorama, 4 metres high, reflected not only Ralph’s known interest in maps but also the excitement created by the first maps of English counties published by Christopher Saxton as an Atlas in 1579.\nTo bring those maps alive the tapestry designer replaced symbols with pictures and added or emphasized details. The gates and palings of parkland stand out; clusters of houses represented villages; windmills, beacons and bridges close to Sheldon properties were added. Towns and some of the large houses, including Sheldon’s own and those of his relatives, were depicted as they really looked. Hills and rivers portrayed the landscape across which wandered roads, which Saxton  had not included. Only trees were drawn out of scale, often shown taller than the church towers and spires.\nThe original design, the same for every tapestry, is made clear by the only intact survival, hanging in the Warwickshire Museum, Warwick.  In the lower right-hand corner were the Sheldon family arms; above was text based on William Camden’s best-selling description of Britain, the Britannia.  The corners of the left-hand side showed the royal arms and a scale and dividers. A variety of designs borrowed from printed sources made up the borders, including flowers, canopies, columns and the figures of Hercules, Temperance and Mercury. A map of Africa was inserted into the lower border.  Doggerel verses described the county and the orientation appeared in each border.\nOwnership and Use\nThe four tapestries, which probably took several years to produce, created a unique decorative scheme, amongst the finest of the time. They may have been damaged when parliamentary forces attacked the royalist Sheldons’ house in the Civil War (1642-48).  Much later, these pieces were deliberately cut from the lower right-hand corner to decorate a fire screen."@en , "Tapestry fragment woven in wool and silk. 16-17 warp threads per in (6-7 per cm). Part of the lower right hand edge of the tapestry map of Oxford and Berkshire, and showing the coat of arms of Ralph Sheldon Senior (1537-1613), and the family motto 'OPTIMUM PATI'. In the border is a wild boar and the tip of Hercules' club which is visible in the right border."@en , "Tapestry fragment woven in wool and silk, Sheldon Tapestry Workshops, possibly made in Warwickshire, ca. 1588"@en ;
